From 882732615cb408d953ff78ea75872621149274da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:29:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 132/159] cidrset: CIDR lists of rule sets as ranges of their
 own

netipx.IPSet keeps a range as two netip.Addr, 48 bytes, and its builder
sorts by reflection and copies the ranges three times more: a rule set
of a million subnets held some 40 MB and took 170 MB (.srs) to 390 MB
(JSON) at its peak on x86.

common/cidrset is the set of the same ranges: IPv4 in a uint64 a range
(8 bytes), IPv6 in 32, sorted and merged in place in the slice they
were gathered in, made once of the size counted; Contains is
netipx.IPSet.Contains. A canonical IPv4 address or prefix is read from
its bytes (Parse4); any other text by netip, as NewIPCIDRItem reads it.

- NewIPCIDRItem builds a cidrset.Set; route/rule makes an item of a
  set (SourceIPRanges, IPRanges, json:"-"); the item gives a TUN that
  routes a rule set a netipx.IPSet made of its ranges.
- domainset.ReadSource reads source_ip_cidr and ip_cidr straight into
  sets; an item netip refuses leaves the rule to sing-box.
- srs.ReadSets, which the loaders of rule sets use, reads the ranges of
  a binary rule set into a set of the size the file tells; srs.Read is
  unchanged.
- The metadata of a rule set counts IPRanges as IP CIDR, as IPSet.

Parse4 is held to netip (300000 random texts, FuzzParse4 15.6 million
inputs); a set to netipx on the ranges and Contains of random lists of
both families, IPv4-mapped IPv6, nested, touching and repeated
prefixes, the edges of both spaces, a zone; ReadSets to Read;
TestReadSourceSameAsStock compares the sets and their descriptions with
what sing-box reads.
